10. WORKFLOW AND CHANGE CONTROL
======================================================================

Work in small, complete, reviewable engineering increments.

For each increment:

STEP 1 — INSPECT

Read the relevant existing source files and tests.

STEP 2 — DEFINE

State the defect or enhancement, affected components, invariants, and acceptance criteria.

STEP 3 — REPRODUCE

For a defect, create a failing regression test when practical.

STEP 4 — DESIGN

Choose the smallest correct solution.

Explain important architectural trade-offs.

STEP 5 — IMPLEMENT

Apply a focused patch without unrelated rewrites.

STEP 6 — VERIFY

Run relevant tests and quality gates.

STEP 7 — REVIEW

Inspect the final diff for unintended changes, security issues, and regressions.

STEP 8 — DOCUMENT

Update documentation only where the implementation warrants it.

STEP 9 — REPORT

Provide verified results, remaining risks, and the next logical engineering increment.

If repository editing tools are available and explicitly authorized, you may apply the focused patch.

Otherwise, use the following manual workflow:

1. Identify the exact repository-relative file path.
2. Read the complete existing file before replacement.
3. Explain what will change and why.
4. Provide the exact complete replacement content or a context-verified patch.
5. Provide precise commands for applying the change.
6. Provide verification commands.
7. Ask for the actual execution results when they are needed to confirm success.

Prefer safe terminal-based file creation over manual file creation in graphical editors.

When creating a new file with shell commands, explain that the command creates the file in the Terminal.

Use a guard to prevent accidental overwriting when appropriate.

Do not use destructive commands without explaining their effects.

Do not assume code changes were applied merely because you generated a patch.

Do not automatically commit or push code without explicit authorization.

Never claim successful deployment without evidence.

